  "overview": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "John Ashcroft (Petitioner)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "97c6498b642d9972ab0b099d0d504cd1760391bc30c03fbee2d51ef7006909fb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-97c6498b642d-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to John Ashcroft (Petitioner), according to John Ashcroft (Petitioner), petitioner John Ashcroft argues that pornography causes substantial, incalculable damage to children and that protecting them is a compelling national interest."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "General Olson",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "97c6498b642d9972ab0b099d0d504cd1760391bc30c03fbee2d51ef7006909fb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-97c6498b642d-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 19
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Petitioner maintains that Congress addressed the problem by adopting the Child Online Protection Act (COPA), which lowers the age threshold to 16, applies to both commercial and noncommercial speech, and incorporates parental\u2011control mechanisms similar to those used in bookstores."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Beeson",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "97c6498b642d9972ab0b099d0d504cd1760391bc30c03fbee2d51ef7006909fb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-97c6498b642d-0001-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 24,
              "start": 24
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Beeson, respondent (American Civil Liberties Union) contends that COPA makes it a crime to communicate protected speech to adults on the World Wide Web, extending the restriction beyond commercial pornographers."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Beeson",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "97c6498b642d9972ab0b099d0d504cd1760391bc30c03fbee2d51ef7006909fb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-97c6498b642d-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 28,
              "start": 28
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Beeson, respondent argues that standards should be national rather than varying by locality."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Beeson",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "97c6498b642d9972ab0b099d0d504cd1760391bc30c03fbee2d51ef7006909fb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-97c6498b642d-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 36,
              "start": 36
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Beeson, respondent further asserts that a national standard for obscenity would be problematic for protected speech, unlike the situation with adult\u2011accessible material."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Beeson",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "97c6498b642d9972ab0b099d0d504cd1760391bc30c03fbee2d51ef7006909fb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-97c6498b642d-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 42,
              "start": 42
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Beeson, respondent notes that Congress created a study commission which found user\u2011based blocking software more effective than credit\u2011card or adult\u2011access code screening."
      }
    ],
